    "text": "Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>B</mi></math>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>C</mi></math> be the two points on the line <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>=0</mo></math>&nbsp;such that&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>B</mi></math> and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>C</mi></math> are symmetric with respect to the origin. Suppose&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi></math> is a point on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></math>&nbsp;such that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8710;</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi></math> is an equilateral triangle. Then, the area of the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8710;</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi></math> is",
